# # shopt
# shopt is a Bash builtin used to set and unset various optional Bash behaviors.
# There are two kinds of options: those set with -s (on) or unset with -u (off),
# and those operated on with -o. The -o options mainly set and show a few specific,
# compatibility-related Bash behaviors.
# ## Common options
# autocd: when enabled, you can cd into a directory by typing just its name.
# cdable_vars: when enabled, you can cd into the directory held in a variable by typing the variable
# name.
# cdspell: when enabled, if the directory name you type has a typo, Bash tries to correct it and cd
# into the right directory.
# checkhash: when enabled, Bash checks that a command path in the hash table is still valid before
# running it.
# cmdhist: when enabled, Bash saves a multi-line command as a single history entry.
# compat31: keeps some behaviors compatible with Bash 3.1.
# compat32: keeps some behaviors compatible with Bash 3.2.
# compat40: keeps some behaviors compatible with Bash 4.0.
# direxpand: when enabled, completion expands a word containing a tilde (~) on the command line to
# the full path name.
# dotglob: when enabled, glob patterns match files starting with a dot.
# execfail: when enabled, the shell continues instead of exiting if the exec system call fails to
# find the command.
# expand_aliases: when enabled, aliases are expanded before running a command.
# extdebug: enables extended debugging features, allowing debugging inside functions and other
# block-level constructs.
# extglob: enables or disables extended glob matching.
# histappend: when enabled, history is appended to the history file when the shell exits instead of
# overwriting it.
# histreedit: when readline is enabled, allows re-editing a failed history substitution.
# histverify: lets the user review and edit a command pulled from history before it runs.
# hostcomplete: when enabled, Tab completion tries to complete host names.
# huponexit: when enabled, the shell sends SIGHUP to all jobs when it exits.
# interactive_comments: when enabled, the shell treats # as the start of a comment.
# lithist: when enabled, the shell keeps embedded newlines of commands in the history.
# login_shell: indicates whether the current shell is a login shell (read-only).
# mailwarn: warns when a mail file has been accessed since the last check.
# nocaseglob: when enabled, glob matching ignores case.
# nocasematch: when enabled, case statement matching ignores case.
# nocd: when enabled, disables the cd command.
# nullglob: when enabled, a glob pattern that matches no files expands to nothing instead of staying
# unexpanded.
# progcomp: enables or disables programmable completion.
# promptvars: when enabled, the shell expands variables in PS1.
# restricted_shell: indicates whether the current shell is a restricted shell (read-only).
# shift_verbose: prints an error when shift exceeds the number of positional parameters.
# sourcepath: when enabled, the shell searches PATH for . filename or source filename.
# xpg_echo: when enabled, echo supports backslash escape sequences.
# ### Common option example (autocd)
# Type `shopt -s autocd` and press Enter
# This turns on autocd. Now you can type a directory name and press Enter to change into it. For
# example:
# ```
# ~/projects $ documents
# ~/documents $
# ```
# If you want autocd on every time you open a terminal, add the command to your ~/.bashrc. Edit
# ~/.bashrc and add this line at the end:
# `shopt -s autocd`
# ## -o options
# Use `shopt -o -p` to see which -o options shopt supports
# shopt is a Bash builtin that shows and sets shell options.
# Different options control various Bash features and behaviors. Here are some common options shopt
# can control:
# allexport: same as set -a, automatically exports all defined variables as environment variables.
# braceexpand: same as set -B, enables brace expansion.
# emacs: same as set -o emacs, uses Emacs-style command line editing.
# errexit: same as set -e, exits the script when a command fails.
# hashall: same as set -h, makes Bash remember command locations to reduce file system lookups.
# histexpand: same as set -H, enables history substitution.
# history: same as set -o history, enables command history.
# ignoreeof: same as set -o ignoreeof, prevents Ctrl-D (EOF) from closing an interactive shell.
# monitor: same as set -o monitor, enables job control.
# noclobber: same as set -o noclobber, prevents redirection from overwriting files.
# noexec: same as set -n, reads commands without running them, used to check script syntax.
# noglob: same as set -f, disables filename glob expansion.
# nolog: same as set -o nolog, prevents function definitions from being saved to history.
# notify: same as set -b, reports job status changes immediately.
# nounset: same as set -u, exits the script when an undefined variable is used.
# pipefail: same as set -o pipefail, makes the whole pipeline return a failure status if any command
# in it fails.

snippet setErrExit exit the script when a command fails
	set -e
	$0

snippet setNounSet exit the script when an undefined variable is used
	set -u
	$0

snippet setPipefail make the whole pipeline return a failure status if any command in it fails
	set -o pipefail
	$0

snippet setExacting strict mode: exit on a failed command, an undefined variable, or a failed pipeline (any command in the pipeline fails)
	set -euo pipefail
	$0
